# CESC 470 — build products only. ONE file at course level, not one per kind:
# hw/, qz/ and exam/ have identical needs and three copies drift.
#
# Verify any change to this file with:  git check-ignore -v <path>

# --- LaTeX intermediates -----------------------------------------------------
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.synctex.gz
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk

# --- flattened self-contained copies for Overleaf ----------------------------
# Generated output. The .tex sources beside them are tracked.
overleaf/

# --- compiled PDFs -----------------------------------------------------------
# Named for what WE build, not "*.pdf" with negations for what we don't.
#
# build_tex.sh writes <basename>.pdf beside each .tex, and every .tex in an
# assignment folder is either pNN_<slug>.tex or <kind>NN_solutions.tex. So these
# two patterns are exactly our build products and nothing else.
#
# A blanket "*.pdf" plus "!HW*.pdf" negations was the old rule here, and it
# fails the wrong way: it silently untracks any source PDF whose name was not
# guessed in advance. This course's sources are "HW1.pdf", "cesc_470.pdf" and
# "Module 01 Introduction to computer technology & ISA (1).pdf" -- only the
# first would have matched. Source material going missing is worse than a stray
# build product showing up in git status, so the rule is a whitelist of our own
# output.
p[0-9][0-9]_*.pdf
*_solutions.pdf

# NOT ignored, deliberately:
#   cesc_470.pdf, Module *.pdf      syllabus and lecture decks -- source
#   hw*/,qz*/,exam*/  HW*.pdf etc.  the instructor's handout -- source, and its
#                                   filename does not have to be guessed
